From: =?UTF-8?q?=EC=8B=AC=EC=83=81=EC=9B=90?= <dev.chamomile@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 09:43:02 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] serial: input while the guest has IER masked raises the
 interrupt (vm-superio 0.8.2)

A backport of upstream's commits 18b08295a82a ("chore(deps): bump
vm-superio to v0.8.2") and 4a28969f6994 ("test: cover serial input
during a console printk flood"), onto v1.18.0, for every host.

A 16550's interrupt line is pending AND enabled. Linux's 8250 console
masks IER for every printk and, restoring it, relies on the UART to
raise RX again for input that came meanwhile (v6.1
drivers/tty/serial/8250/8250_port.c serial8250_console_write: "The
receive handling will happen properly because the receive ready bit
will still be set"). vm-superio 0.8.1 raised RDA only when a byte was
enqueued (serial.rs enqueue_raw_bytes, received_data_interrupt) and an
IER write only stored the value. So input that arrived in the masked
window stayed in the FIFO until the next byte came.

This is what made a restored smp guest's first command wait for the
next input on wick's Linux CI (runs 43486495448, 43521200989,
43528910212):
- Each restore in a new process loads a new VMGenID. About 11 ms after
  the resume the guest prints "random: crng reseeded due to virtual
  machine fork" (v6.1 drivers/char/random.c add_vmfork_randomness), which
  keeps IER masked for about 1.3 ms. That is when the harness sends its
  command.
- In the reproducer (boot-test.sh --stage restores, run 43528972093),
  2 of 36 restores were late, 0 of 6 same-process resumes, which print
  nothing.
- KVM's trace of a late one: IER written 0 at 11.156 ms and 5 at
  12.482 ms, then no serial interrupt for 35 s, until the next line.
  Then the guest read both lines' 32 bytes at once.

vm-superio 0.8.2 re-evaluates the pending interrupts on an IER write. It
raises RDA when the FIFO is not empty, and THRE, the THR being always
empty in the model. Guest-visible: Linux's start-up probe
(serial8250_do_startup) no longer marks the port UART_BUG_TXEN, and
sends from the THRE interrupt. Upstream Firecracker ships the same.
vm-superio has no non-dev dependencies, so the crate builds unchanged on
Linux, macOS and Windows.

Tests:
- New, wick's, need no guest and run on every host:
  - devices::legacy::serial::tests::test_input_while_ier_is_masked_is_signalled_when_ier_comes_back
  - devices::legacy::serial::tests::test_enabling_the_thr_empty_interrupt_raises_it
  Both fail on 0.8.1 ("no interrupt for the waiting input", "no
  interrupt for the empty THR") and pass on 0.8.2 (macOS 27.2).
- Upstream's test_serial_input_during_console_printk (both variants), on
  macOS 27.2: 10 of 10 trials lost on 0.8.1, passes on 0.8.2.
  test_serial_io.py passes as before otherwise (test_serial_dos needs
  root).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
